From 0b4ecfe8c908a44ac6606f058c2df6cfa3a8ec6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 00:55:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 33/33] lib/streamaggr: place sample_timestamps rounds once,
 write each interval once and complete

sample_timestamps put a sample by what the aggregator's flush had already
written, which differed between outputs and changed in the middle of a round:
a late round processed while a flush walked the outputs went to one interval
in some outputs and to the next in others, and the cost and the tokens of one
round came out short in one minute and over in the next.

Where a sample goes now follows from its round alone, the same in every output:

- A sample belongs to the interval [end - interval, end) its scrape timestamp
  falls in, so a round scraped on a boundary, as scrape_align_interval has
  them, goes to the interval that starts there.
- Processed before that interval is written, it is written with it, at its end.
- Processed after, while it is the interval written last, it is the tail of a
  round crossing the boundary: the next flush writes the interval again, at the
  same timestamp, complete; deduplication keeps the bigger value. A tail that
  would make it smaller -- negative, or after sum_samples_total started its
  total again -- goes to the next interval instead.
- Processed later than that, it goes to the first interval not written when
  its round's first sample was processed, decided once per scrape timestamp, so
  the rest of the round goes there too. Only a round still being processed a
  whole interval after that has its later samples go further.
- Rounds scraped before the aggregator started go to its first interval; a
  timestamp ahead of the clock counts as now.

Nothing is dropped however far behind the aggregator is, and an output holds a
fixed number of sums: no back-off milliseconds, no watermark, no latest-written
tracking across outputs. sample_timestamps is refused with ignore_old_samples,
which drops the late rounds it places.

Tests: a named test per rule; a round processed while a flush walks the
outputs, on time and late, stays in one interval (a hook between entries
pushes it there); a randomized test, 20 seeds x both outputs x 1m and 7m x with
and without flushes running over, checks that every interval has cost three
times the tokens, totals are exact and nothing is written early, and without
overruns every interval against a reference model of the rules. Each of the 11
rules, broken in turn, fails at least one test. -race passes.

Cost: 163 bytes per output series, two intervals pending or one (032: 147 and
188, option off about 131); a flush of 20 targets x 2000 keys 7.7 ms (off 7.5).
At 37% of the full ACG response with the 102 production rules, 9.5M samples a
minute: no flush timeouts, lag 0.2s, every minute exact in two separate rules,
heap +250 MB over the option off.
